# OptiAI — handoff

Written for the next engineer (or agent) picking this up cold.
State as of 2026-09-23. Nothing has been pushed to GitHub yet — see **Blocked** below.

---

## What it is

One local gateway in front of every AI provider. You point your CLI tools
(Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Cline…) at OptiAI instead of at a provider, and it
routes, prices and records every request. There is also a web UI for chatting,
comparing models and reading your usage back.

## Stack

| Part | What |
|---|---|
| `frontend/` | Next.js 16 (Turbopack), React 19, Tailwind v4. Port **20181** |
| `backend/` | Express. Port **20180**. Thin service layer over a vendored router |
| `backend/9router/` | **Vendored third-party router.** Provider executors, OAuth, credential rotation, SSE. Treat as upstream — patch sparingly and comment why |
| Storage | SQLite at `backend/.data/` — also holds provider OAuth tokens and API keys in readable form. Gitignored; do not copy between machines |

```bash
npm run app        # both, concurrently
npm run backend    # just the API
npm run frontend   # just the UI
```

Two endpoint families:

- `/api/*` — the web UI's own API (traced, typed, OptiAI-shaped)
- `/v1/chat/completions`, `/v1/messages`, `/v1/responses` — raw OpenAI/Anthropic-compatible gateway for real CLI tools. Auth enforced: `Authorization: Bearer <key>` from Connect, 401 without

Both funnel into the same `routeChat`, so behaviour cannot drift between them.

---

## Blocked right now

**The initial commit has never pushed.** GitHub push protection rejects it over
four Google OAuth client ID/secret pairs inside the vendored router
(`providers/registry/gemini.js`, `gemini-cli.js`, `antigravity.js`,
`providers/shared.js`).

They are **not our secrets**. They are Google *installed-application* clients
that ship inside Gemini CLI and Antigravity — extractable from any install, and
under RFC 8252 a native-app client secret is not confidential. The vendored code
labels them `public CLI client` itself.

Decision taken: allowlist them through GitHub's unblock URLs rather than strip
them, so Gemini/Antigravity keep working with no configuration. Those URLs are
in the rejected-push output and expire, so a fresh push may be needed to
regenerate them.

Audited before publishing: `backend/.data/` is untracked, no gateway key, no
private keys, no provider API keys in tracked files. The only other
credential-shaped values are Windsurf/Codeium **Firebase web keys**, which are
public by design and which GitHub does not flag.

Unresolved and worth a look: this repo vendors 9Router's source. Check its
licence permits redistribution and that attribution is where it needs to be.

---

## Known issues

1. **`data: [DONE]` is appended to non-streaming `/v1` responses.** Valid JSON
   followed by an SSE terminator. Affects *every* model, not just combos — a
   strict OpenAI client will fail to parse. Pre-existing, lives in the vendored
   router, not yet fixed.
2. **NVIDIA 404s intermittently.** The model id is valid (it is in NVIDIA's own
   `/v1/models`), and three consecutive calls succeeded. The router reads the
   404 as a rate-limit signal and opens a ~1 minute breaker, during which
   everything fails instantly — a CLI retrying inside that window is guaranteed
   to fail. Give combos a second model so one breaker cannot take the whole
   combo down.
3. **`z-ai/glm-5.3` fails across all three NVIDIA keys.** Fallback is on by
   default for any error, so all three were tried. Either the id is wrong for
   this provider or no key has access.
4. **Image attachments are unverified end-to-end.** The router has no
   multimodal code; it forwards the OpenAI `image_url` content array as-is.
   Should work on OpenAI-compatible providers, never proven — no vision model
   is connected.
5. **Tool calling through the router is untested.**
6. **Pricing and capabilities are curated, not reported.** The catalog returns
   only `id`, `name`, `provider` for all 105 models. Rates and capability badges
   come from `frontend/src/lib/catalog/modelFacts.ts`, matched by id pattern,
   first match wins. Unknown rates render an em dash, never `$0.00` — on a cost
   tool "free" and "unknown" must not look alike. That file is the one place to
   correct a rate.

---

## Gotchas found the hard way

Each of these cost real debugging time. They are not obvious from the code.

**Tailwind: a default class plus a caller override is a coin flip.** A component
with `h-6` in its base classes that appends `className` gets `h-6 … h-5` on one
element, and CSS resolves that by **stylesheet order, not attribute order** — so
the caller silently loses. `Wordmark` takes a numeric `height` prop for exactly
this reason. Do not "fix" it back to a class.

**`border-beam` sets `overflow: hidden` on its root, always** — not only while
animating. Anything that escapes its bounds gets clipped. The model picker
dropdown is portalled to `document.body` with measured fixed coordinates
because of this. Portalled menus are not DOM descendants of their trigger, so
click-outside must check the menu too.

**`next/image` 400s on SVG** (`"url" parameter is valid but image type is not
allowed`) unless `unoptimized` is set. A build passing proves nothing here —
images resolve at runtime.

**A sticky element cannot be centred with `left-1/2`.** `left` only constrains
sticky during horizontal scroll. Use a sticky wrapper that does the centring.

**`useMemo` is not for side effects.** React may skip or re-run it at will.
Re-seeding dialog state in a memo meant edits landed only when something else
forced a render.

**Holding a captured object goes stale.** A page that stores `editing = combo`
keeps showing the old one after anything else edits it. Store the id, derive
the object from the live list.

**Combo names are case-sensitive and may not contain a slash.** Lookup is
`WHERE name = ?` with no `COLLATE NOCASE`; a name with a slash is parsed as
`provider/model` and never reaches the combo lookup. Both rules are enforced
server-side.

**`localhost` and `127.0.0.1` are the same endpoint** and compare unequal as
strings. CLI "Configured" detection normalises loopback spellings — without
that, a correctly configured tool reads as not configured.

**`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` does nothing to a table that already exists.** A
new column needs its own `ALTER TABLE`, guarded, in `services/store.js`.

---

## Where things live

| Concern | File |
|---|---|
| Model rates, tags, capabilities | `frontend/src/lib/catalog/modelFacts.ts` |
| Attachments, long-paste → file, content blocks | `frontend/src/lib/attachments.ts` |
| Per-thread streaming (several chats at once) | `frontend/src/hooks/useStreams.tsx` |
| Follow-the-stream scrolling | `frontend/src/hooks/useStickToBottom.ts` |
| Combo CRUD + name rules | `backend/services/models.js` |
| Test verdicts, winning API key | `backend/services/modelTests.js` |
| Per-request tracing (both paths) | `backend/services/trace.js`, `backend/api/index.js` |
| CLI config detection (read-only) | `backend/services/cli.js` |
| Schema + migrations | `backend/services/store.js` |

Combos are **server-side**: the router resolves a combo by name from the
database, so a browser-only combo can never route. That is why CRUD is wired at
`/api/models/combos`.

Model test verdicts are filed under the key that **answered**, not the one you
clicked Test on — the router falls through keys on failure and stops at the
first success, so those differ whenever a provider has several keys.

---

## Conventions

- Semantic CSS variables (`var(--text)`, `var(--surface)`), never raw hex in components
- The logo gradient is `--grad-logo`; `--grad-brand` is the lighter variant for sitting behind UI text
- `frontend/AGENTS.md` carries a warning that this Next.js version differs from training data — read `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before assuming an API
- Comments explain *why*, especially where something looks wrong but is deliberate

## Verifying a change

`npm run build` passing is necessary and not sufficient. Several real bugs this
session compiled cleanly and failed at runtime. Start both servers and exercise
the actual path — the SVG 400, the dropdown clipping and the `localhost` vs
`127.0.0.1` mismatch all built fine.

---

## Added 2026-09-23 (network map, time-bucketed chart, split view)

**Network map on Usage → Overview.** `backend/services/network.js` exposes
`GET /api/network` (every provider that has a connection or needs none, with
stored health) and `POST /api/network/check/:provider` (live probe: every
active key through the router's own connection test, or one 1-token
completion for a no-auth provider — that one *writes* a model-test verdict).
`frontend/src/components/network/ProviderTopology.tsx` draws OptiAI in the
middle and probes every node on mount, staggered 350 ms apart; lines run the
blue→violet gradient while in flight and settle green/red. Clicking a node
shows passing models (with the key that answered), keys, and recent prompts.
`RequestsRail.tsx` beside it is the compact prompt log, fed by `/usage/traces`.

**Chart.** `GET /api/usage/chart` now returns `{ period, bucketMs, points[] }`
with a `ts` per bucket, computed in `services/usage.js` from `usageHistory`
(the vendored `getChartData` is kept as `chartLegacy`). The UI used to send
`"1d"` for Today, which the vendored function did not know and treated as 60
days. Buckets: 1 h for today/24h, 3 h up to 3 days, 6 h up to 14, else 1 day.
`AreaChart` lays the x-axis out by time: hours inside a day, dated midnights
with hour marks between them across a week, dates only beyond that.

**Split view.** `hooks/useSplitView.tsx` holds the pane target
(`{kind:"route",href}` or `{kind:"thread",threadId}`) and the width ratio in
localStorage. Sidebar rows and chats are HTML5-draggable; `AppShell` shows a
two-zone drop target while a drag is in progress (left = open in main, right =
open beside). `components/layout/SplitPane.tsx` renders the page component
directly via `PaneRouter.tsx` — it is not a second Next router, so dynamic
routes (`/providers/[id]`, `/connect/[tool]`) navigate the main column when
clicked inside a pane. Chat reads `usePane()` for its thread id so two
conversations can sit side by side; the global selection is untouched.
Close is the round X at the pane's bottom right; the divider drags and
double-clicks back to 50/50.

### Same day, second pass

**OptiAI thinks with a model now.** `backend/services/ai.js` picks the fastest
model that last tested clean and runs one completion through `routeChatJson`.
Three routes: `POST /api/ai/rank` (Models and Skills AI search — the page
sends its candidate list, gets top 5 ids with reasons), `POST /api/ai/optify`
(Projects "OptiFy" — title/description in, model/skill/plugin ids out) and
`POST /api/ai/analyze` (Analytics "Re-evaluate" — totals plus the unusual
prompts in, summary/findings/suspicious prompts out; last result cached per
period in memory and in the browser). Reasoning models spend the token budget
thinking before the JSON, so budgets are 2.5–4k and `parseLoose` repairs a
reply cut off mid-string rather than failing. `[DONE]` suffix handled there too.

**Layout is container-driven.** `<main>` and the split pane's scroller are
`@container`; pages use `@2xl:`/`@3xl:`/`@5xl:` grid variants instead of
viewport breakpoints, so a page in a half-width pane stacks the way a narrow
window would. Est. cost tile shows two decimals under `@5xl`.

**Network map drills in.** Click a provider: it becomes the hub and its tested
models are the ring (green passed, red failed). Back arrow returns. Topology
re-reads every 20 s; the request rail every 15 s; the Usage header Refresh
button re-reads everything and re-probes. Hub used `.grad-border`, which sets
`position: relative` and overrode `absolute` — `.net-grad` paints only.

**UI.** `components/ui/Select.tsx` replaces the native select everywhere
(portalled menu, brand scrollbar). Modal's red light is the close control.
ModelGrid: a failed test only reddens the border; selection stays independent.
Analytics gauge is a 240° arc sized by CSS. `BulkConnectDialog` adds many API
keys to one provider from CSV/paste or typed rows, one request per key.
